# Quadratic Forms Across Fields: An Interactive Journey

A quadratic equation may keep its coefficients while changing its solution set. Begin with the hands-on zero finder below: it plots the arithmetic question behind isotropy for $Q(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2-z^2$.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The bounded exact search implements the same question over rational vectors. Finding a vector certifies isotropy; an unsuccessful finite search is only evidence, not a proof.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

## Coordinates move; quadratic geometry does not

An invertible change of variables can make a formula look different without changing whether nonzero zeros exist. Experiment with the shear, then compare it with the generated grid illustration.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the isometry theorem and proof idea</summary>

**Isometry Invariance Theorem.** If an invertible linear map $e$ satisfies $Q'(e(v))=Q(v)$ for every $v$, then $Q$ is isotropic exactly when $Q'$ is isotropic. A zero moves forward by $e$ and backward by $e^{-1}$; invertibility prevents a nonzero witness from becoming zero.

{{algorithm:1}}

</details>

## A larger field can add solutions, but cannot erase one

The form $x^2+y^2$ has no nonzero rational zero, yet over the complex numbers $(1,i)$ is a zero. Explore the transition directly.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal scalar extension and witness transport</summary>

For a field extension $L/K$, vectors enter the enlarged space $L\otimes_K V$ through $v\mapsto 1\otimes v$. If $v
e0$ and $Q(v)=0$, then $1\otimes v
e0$ and $Q_L(1\otimes v)=0$. Hence isotropy ascends, while anisotropy descends contrapositively.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:1}}

</details>

## The local–global horizon

Every number field has characteristic zero, so the transport theorem applies to all of its extensions, including completions. This gives the easy half of the local–global story: a global zero yields a zero everywhere locally. The converse belongs to the Hasse–Minkowski theorem and needs arithmetic reciprocity.

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Background and further reading</summary>

Learn more from the [Encyclopedia of Mathematics entry on quadratic forms](https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Quadratic_form), the [Hasse–Minkowski theorem overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hasse%E2%80%93Minkowski_theorem), and the [Stacks Project discussion of tensor products](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00CV). The central distinction is between transporting one known witness and constructing a global witness from separate local ones.

</details>
